User: How do tariff rates affect terms of trade between trading partners?
Assistant: Tariffs affect terms of trade through standard optimal tariff theory, whereby countries with market power can exploit their monopsony position in world markets. The theoretical framework demonstrates that a country facing upward-sloping foreign export supply curves can improve its welfare by imposing tariffs that equate the marginal cost of imports to their marginal value in consumption and production. The optimal tariff rate equals the inverse of the foreign export supply elasticity, suggesting higher optimal rates for products where trading partners have limited alternative markets. Empirical evidence from gravity models confirms that larger economies face less elastic export supply curves and thus have greater scope for terms-of-trade manipulation. While retaliatory tariffs may erode these gains, game-theoretic models show that credible tariff threats can induce trading partners to maintain more favorable trade policies. The focus on static deadweight losses often ignores dynamic benefits from protecting industries with learning-by-doing externalities or strategic importance.

